<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Contras</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1980s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Contras were right-wing rebel groups that fought the Sandinista government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> in the 1980s. Formed largely around former members of Anastasio Somoza's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">National Guard</a>, they were armed, trained and funded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and operated from camps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>. Their war cost tens of thousands of lives, and secret US funding of the Contras led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>–Contra affair.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/contras.jpg" alt="Contras" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tiomono · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Contra_commandas_1987.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Contras, from the Spanish contrarrevolucionarios, were anti-communist rebels who fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandinista-national-liberation-front">Sandinista National Liberation Front</a> (FSLN) and the Junta of National Reconstruction, which took power after the Nicaraguan Revolution of 1979. In July 1979 the FSLN took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a> after weeks of heavy fighting, and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-debayle">Anastasio Somoza Debayle</a> fled the country. Dissidents of the new government and members of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">National Guard</a> formed groups that met and established the Contras in 1980.</p><p>They were not a single body but three elements of Nicaraguan society: former National Guardsmen and other right-wing figures; anti-Somoza activists who had supported the revolution but felt betrayed by the Sandinistas, such as Édgar Chamorro and José Francisco Cardenal; and Nicaraguans who had stayed out of the revolution but opposed the Sandinistas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In September 1981 the CIA and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>'s intelligence agency persuaded the 15 September Legion, the Nicaraguan Democratic Union and several smaller groups to merge as the Nicaraguan Democratic Force (FDN). It was led by the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">National Guard</a> colonel Enrique Bermúdez as military commander and the businessman Adolfo Calero as head of its political directorate, and was based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>. In April 1982 Edén Pastora, a former Sandinista, organised the Sandinista Revolutionary Front in the south and declared war on the government. In June 1985 most groups reorganised as the United Nicaraguan Opposition, and in May 1987 the Nicaraguan Resistance was organised on similar lines.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> provided military assistance and funds. On 4 January 1982 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a> signed a secret directive giving the CIA authority to support the Contras with 19 million dollars in military aid. After Congress blocked further aid, the Reagan administration raised funds from third countries and private sources through a National Security Council operation run by Lieutenant Colonel Oliver North, which culminated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>–Contra affair of 1986–1987. In 1986 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> found that the United States had violated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a> by supporting the Contras and mining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>'s harbours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Americas Watch and Human Rights Watch accused the Contras of attacks on civilians, kidnapping, torture and executions, and Human Rights Watch called them major and systematic violators of the laws of armed conflict. Calero denied that his forces deliberately targeted civilians, and the Reagan administration contended that the Contras did not deliberately attack them. After a cutoff in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> military support, five Central American presidents, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-ortega">Daniel Ortega</a>, agreed with the Contras that voluntary demobilisation should begin in early December 1989, ahead of the elections of February 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Contras” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1980s the country hosted US forces at the Palmerola (Soto Cano) air base and camps of the Nicaraguan <b>Contras</b>, while the army’s Battalion 3-16 carried out disappearances of suspected leftists.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sandinista revolution of 1978–1979 destroyed the Guard, and in the 1980s the Sandinista Popular Army fought the US-backed <b>Contra</b> rebels in a war that killed some 30,000 people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Military</a></li></ul></section>
